  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/4/4a/Fantastic_Four_Vol_1_25.png",
  "plot_summary": "Reed urges Ben to drink a formula he's accidentally stumbled upon that will permanently change him back to Ben Grimm, but the Thing smashes it because Alicia likes him as the Thing.&nbsp; He only wants a formula that will allow him to change back and forth!&nbsp; Alicia then arrives with a newspaper that announces that the Avengers have returned to the US after replacing the Hulk with Captain America.&nbsp; But what happened to the Hulk?\n\nAt that moment, out in New Mexico, the Hulk catches a ride on a truck (after demanding its drivers to \"Let me ride in your truck, and I'll let you live!\").&nbsp; But as they drive away, he suddenly reverts back to \"Bob\" Banner and hops out at the next stop, \"heading for the hills!\"&nbsp; (He makes it to his underground lab.)\n\nNot far away, the Avengers are examining some of the wreckage left behind by the Hulk's takeover of the truck.&nbsp; Captain America has now joined their ranks, and they've traveled out to New Mexico in search of the Hulk.&nbsp; But the trail runs cold, and they're forced to return to New York City.\n\nMeanwhile, \"Bob\" Banner has changed back to the Hulk, and finding himself surrounded by the scientific machinery owned by \"Bob\" Banner, he decides to smash it to prevent himself from ever becoming the puny human Banner ever again.&nbsp; He even decides to empty his pockets, but finds a newspaper clipping that details how the Avengers have replaced the Hulk in their ranks with the newly-revived Captain America.\n\nThe Hulk is outraged, and he decides to head back to New York City to \"destroy the Avengers <i>forever</i>!\"&nbsp; Even though his legs make \"powerful, distance-swallowing leaps,\" it still takes him 24 hours to finally reach the East Coast.\n\nBy the time the Hulk arrives, the scene shifts back to the Baxter Building, where Reed has suddenly taken ill, so Johnny takes the Fantasti-Car to go fetch the doctor, but he spots the Hulk below, smashing up a building.&nbsp; He takes off as the Torch to investigate, but is bested by the Hulk, who leaves him since he's actually looking for the Avengers.\n\nThe Hulk's rampage causes the police commissioner to summon the FF for help, but Ben says that Reed is sick.&nbsp; Johnny is gone, but Reed, who can't go himself, urges Ben and Sue to go after the Hulk, warning Ben that the Hulk is far stronger -- he must use his wits!&nbsp; They get to the Hulk just as Johnny recovers, and Sue tries to protect him from the Hulk with her force shield, but the strain is too much, and she loses consciousness.\n\nThe Thing finally confronts the Hulk face-to-face.&nbsp; Though they've met before (in 1962's [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12]), the Hulk is not interested in the Thing.&nbsp; He wants only the Avengers and intends to smash his way through anything that gets in his way!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=And so it is that \"the two titans confront each other... heralding the start of one of the greatest hand-to-hand battles in recorded history!\"]\n\nAnd indeed, the two combatants go at each other for page after page after page... while a helpless Reed watches the action on television, knowing that he could help, but completely unable to do anything.&nbsp; The folks on <i>Yancy Street</i> are paying attention, and they even try to <i>help</i> the Thing, saying, \"We're not lettin' anyone <i>else</i> pulverize our favorite whippin' boy!\"\n\nBut as the fight progresses, the Thing notes that he's growing tired while it seems the Hulk continues to grow ever-stronger!&nbsp; Meanwhile, back at the Baxter Building, Sue has finally found the doctor, who had warned Reed against working so much with the \"strange microbes\" and \"untested formulae\" (that Reed exposed him to while working on a formula to help Ben).&nbsp; As a result, Reed has caught a virus and needs complete rest.&nbsp; Sue says \"Oh, Reed... my darling... I... never realized how much I love you... <i>need</i> you!\"\n\nBack at the battle, the Hulk finally bests Ben and turns his attention back to the city, as it's the <i>Avengers</i> he really wants!\n[BR] In his eyes, the Thing has been nothing but a minor hindrance.\n\nHowever, the now-abandoned Thing pulls himself up, astonished that he's been beaten, but he'll have none of it!&nbsp; Maybe he needed the pounding to make him realize he's not unbeatable.\n\n\"Well, like my dear ol' Aunt Petunia used to say...'You can only die once!'\n[BR] And that's the <i>only</i> way he'll stop me now -- by <i>killin'</i> me!!\"\n\n[CONTINUED]",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "It wasn't the <i>first</i> Thing/Hulk battle (<i>that</i> happened back in [LINK:1962:FF_12|FF #12]), but it was certainly the most <i>extensive</i> one to date!&nbsp; And one look at that cover showing the Hulk absolutely <i>towering</i> over the Thing suggests that the fate of Ben Grimm seems very much in doubt!&nbsp; The so-called \"<b>BATTLE OF THE CENTURY!</b>\" was an [SPAN: severity=success; text=excellent way to attract readers], so kudos to Jack Kirby and company.\n\n[SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; text=I was hooked... even <i>before</i> I opened the magazine!]\n\n[ASSET: left | AvengersInTheNews | width=27%] As an early Marvel reader (both for real and in [PAGE: scenario] we're now all trapped in, <i>wink</i>), every sign has pointed to something like this happening.&nbsp; The Avengers essentially battled the Hulk over the course of their first three issues (which I wrote about in my [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01] review).\n[BR]\n[BR] You just have to admire the way Stan and Jack <i>immediately</i> tied the events of <i>this</i> issue to what had just happened over in <i>that</i> title just one publication week earlier (which is, of course, the dramatic return of the genuine Captain America in [LINK:1964:AVEN_4|AVEN #4|2027-04-25]).\n[BR]\n[BR] If the cover hadn't been indication enough, the story seems determined to follow up on that ongoing thread, except now in the pages of <i>Fantastic Four</i>!\n\nTo that I say:&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Sign me up!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Amazing that I'm already salivating, and I'm still only on the top of page 3!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: right | NoFormulaForMe | width=19%] What's even more amazing is that, even <i>before</i> Sue reads aloud the newspaper for the benefit of the blind Alicia, the first couple of pages had featured a wary Thing smashing Reed's latest formula aimed at permanently transforming him back to the human Ben Grimm, <i>despite</i> the fact that it would mean the loss of \"one fourth\" of the team's fighting strength.\n[BR]\n[BR] Even amidst the build-up leading to this \"Battle of the Century,\" Stan and Jack don't forget to deliver characterization to the title team -- [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=well done!]\n\nWhat Reed seems to have forgotten, however, is the promise he made to his friend on page 10 of last April's [LINK:1963:FF_16|FF #16], where Ben spells it out for him very plainly --\"...forget about tryin' to change me back...\"&nbsp; Ben asks him there to devote his energies instead to trying to help Alicia see again -- which, to Reed's credit, he at least <i>tries</i> to in [LINK:1963:FF_19|FF #19|2026-12-04]).\n\nWhile it's true that Reed sees the truth -- that Alicia loves <i>Ben Grimm</i>, and not just the rocky Thing (as Ben supposes), it's likely Stan and Jack who have forgotten this prior conversation from nine months earlier -- even if readers (or someone like me) haven't, <i>heh</i>.&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(I mean, <i>come on</i>!&nbsp; Stan can't even remember Banner's first <i>name</i>, much less what happened in this magazine back in April!)]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Did I mention that I'm still only on <b>page 3</b>?]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Already, I'm rubbing my hands together in anticipation!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | LetYouLive | width=20%] Of course, not everything was sunshine and roses, as that same page features the Hulk hijacking a truck out in New Mexico, demanding a lift, and even threatening its drivers with the line [SPAN: severity=error; text=\"Let me ride in your truck and I'll let you live!\"]&nbsp; <b><i>Geez!</i></b>\n[BR]\n[BR] I know the Hulk is constantly being hunted, and he certainly has no love of \"puny humans,\" but I don't recall him ever threatening to <i>kill</i> a couple in this manner, [SPAN: severity=error; text=so this strikes me as wildly out of character.]\n[BR]\n[BR] Furthermore, Marvel has already established that the Hulk can cover vast distances simply by making giant leaps, so [SPAN: severity=warning; text=what did he need a lift for in the <i>first</i> place?]&nbsp; This is <i>literally</i> shown shortly afterward, where on page 5, he states that his leg muscles \"push me hard enough to leap for <i>miles</i>!\"\n\nAlong the way, Stan reminds everyone how he's forgotten the name of Hulk's alter-ego (perhaps writing this issue right around the same time as last month's [LINK:1963:AVEN_3|AVEN #3|2027-03-01]).&nbsp; The Hulk's random transformation back to \"Bob\" Banner helps serves as a refresher for readers out there who might not have been familiar with the character.\n\nAs shocking as it seems that the writer can't even seem to get the character's name right, putting myself in the context of someone trying to understand the publishing environment of the day, I can <i>kind of</i> see what Stan and Jack are trying to do, <i>especially</i> given that it's been an entire <i>year</i> since the Hulk had his own magazine.&nbsp; And even though he's appeared in the Avengers more recently, none of those adventures were <i>ever intended</i> to focus solely on the Hulk, so it is still <i>conceivable</i> that some FF readers may not have been familiar with the character.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: As for the \"Bob\" snafu, which started in <i>Avengers</i> #3, Stan's mistake was pointed out to him in an upcoming letters page, and he was forced to retroactively give Dr. Banner the full name of \"Robert Bruce Banner.\"&nbsp; Thus, he saved face in the end, but I'm <i>sure</i> that it was actually just a mistake, <i>heh</i>.]\n\nAnd speaking of mistakes, page 5 has the Avengers out in New Mexico, surveying the damage the Hulk caused to the hijacked truck.\n\nThere, we find Captain America saying that, [SPAN: severity=info; text=\"Only the <i>Hulk</i> could have done this!\"]\n[ASSET: center | AvengersAppear | width=large]\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; text=How on Earth would <i>he</i> know?&nbsp; He's never even <i>met</i> the Hulk!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(something he's very quick to point out to Rick Jones on page 10 of this month's Avengers issue)]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(And <i>curse</i> you, Jack Kirby, for once again showing Iron Man flying with his knees bent!&nbsp; <b><i>Arrgh!</i></b>)]\n\nAgain... I <i>get it</i> -- it's a story, and the intent is to build up to something, particularly in light of what seems to be coming next month.&nbsp; I suppose it is important to show the Avengers out engaging in their favorite pastime -- getting in a little [SPAN: severity=success; fontsize=larger; fonttype=bold; text=Hulk hunting] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(copyright pending)].&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=warning; fonttype=italic; text=(Still...)]\n\nFrom a storytelling perspective, what is largely going unnoticed is how the Hulk and his history with the Avengers quietly consumes all of pages 4-6 (and the majority of page 3 as well).&nbsp; I must say that having the Hulk become outraged that the Avengers have chosen to replace him \"someone named Captain America\" was clever, as it gives the Hulk an actual motive to return to NYC.\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; text=If only he realized this isn't an issue of the <b><i>Avengers</i></b> but rather the <b><i>Fantastic Four!</i></b>]&nbsp; [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(heh...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nThere was just one more thing for Stan and Jack to do... which was to eliminate the other members of the Fantastic Four, all to force the Thing into solo action against the arriving Hulk, who is now in the middle of a rampage through New York!\n\n[SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Everyone run for their lives!]\n\n[ASSET: left | ReedIsIll | width=12%] [ASSET: right | HesJustAKid | width=12%] And this is <i>exactly</i> what Stan and Jack proceed to do, though it takes a full 4 pages,\n[BR]\n[BR] * first taking Reed out of the equation by having him be sick (page 7),\n[BR] * knocking out the Human Torch simply because he is no match for the Hulk (page 8),\n[BR] * and finally forcing the Invisible Girl to expend her force field energy by trying to protect her brother against the Hulk.\n[BR]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=That leaves just the Thing... <i>exactly</i> as the co-creators planned....]\n\nSay what you want about the absolutely unabashed and [SPAN: severity=warning; fontsize=large; fonttype=bold; text=BLATANT] plot device ([SPAN: severity=error; text=it's big enough to drive a truck through]), but it nevertheless  deposits readers squarely at the halfway point of the story, all of them [SPAN: severity=success; text=clamoring] for the main event, this [SPAN: fontsize=larger; text=\"<b>BATTLE OF THE CENTURY!</b>\"]\n\n[SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=From this point forward, only the Thing stands between the Hulk and the populace!]\n[ASSET: center | ButterUpYourPopcorn | width=large]\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Readers, <i>this</i> is where you pause to go butter up that popcorn!&nbsp; This baby is about to get <i>lively</i>!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n\n[ASSET: left | IEatPizzaWithThatHand | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: left | HeGoingAboutItWrong | width=12%] [ASSET: right | MeanwhileOnYancyStreet | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | HowDoesHeDoIt | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: left | SueNeedsReed | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: center | YouCanOnlyDieOnce | width=large]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nIndeed, Jack Kirby's battle sequence is largely top-notch, as anyone might expect, with Stan's dialog between the two being also about what you'd expect, so no problems with that aspect of the story.&nbsp; The thing that prevents this issue from rising above just \"Fine\" is the convenient setup of Reed's sudden illness and inability to lead the team into battle.&nbsp; In fact, the story even hints that, had he been there, he could have coordinated their attack to properly subdue the Hulk.&nbsp; But this very sudden illness causes the team to be separated and thus rendered ineffective -- and it seems to be the only reason Ben is thrust out there to do battle alone.\n\n\neader, you knew this was going to happen (as you also know an inevitable Thor/Hulk battle will one day follow -- in the eventual <i>Journey Into Mystery</i> #112), and the \"next issue\" blurb promises that the action is only going to intensify as \"the Avengers join the battle!\"\n\n\n\nFinally... <i>um</i>... I think it's fair to ask:\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Whatever happened to the Fantasti-Car that Johnny abandoned on page 7?]\n[HANG] Is it still hovering up there above the city?\n\nAs for me, I have a couple of guesses:\n[HANG] 1) We're supposed to assume it was programmed to return to the Baxter Building on auto-pilot, OR...\n[HANG] 2) Readers were never supposed to ask this question!\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(My money is on the <i>latter</i>!&nbsp; What <i>other</i> explanation is there?)]\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nYou have to wonder what the plotting session between Stan and Jack amounted to.&nbsp; Was it much more than \"Hey Jack, let's have the Thing and Hulk battle for most of the issue.&nbsp; Have them fight for a really long time!&nbsp; Make sure to end it with the Thing pulling himself off the ground, determined to finish the fight!&nbsp; I'll make up the rest!\n\nThis left Jack free to fill up half a story with little other than all-out-action, and... <i>well</i>... [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=bold; text=he's really good at that!]\n\n\n\nWe admire Ben's courage and tenacity, as he's obviously up against a more powerful opponent than perhaps he's ever had to face, and that's all well and good, but the surrounding circumstances that put him in that position to begin with are suspect and prevent the issue from elevating to a higher status.&nbsp; It remains just \"Fine\" in the end.&nbsp; (But hey, we really <i>do</i> want to make sure we catch that next issue!&nbsp; The Avengers?&nbsp; The Hulk?&nbsp; The Thing?&nbsp; Nice, but we sure as heck hope the actual Fantastic Four make an appearance or two as well!)",
  "side_note": [
    "[HANG0] * The splash page indicates that this tale is \"double-length.\"&nbsp; Curious, since it seems to be about the same length as any other full-length magazine, so perhaps they meant that the tale was so long, it had to spill over into the next issue.\n\n[HANG0] * Page 5 indicates that the Avengers have arrived in New Mexico in a helicopter.&nbsp; While it's not <i>explicitly</i> stated that they travelled all the way from New York City in the helicopter, the story seems to <i>imply</i> that, but that does lead me to scratch my head.&nbsp; While it's true that the 1964 record of 1,348 miles was set later that year, most helicopters at the time had a maximum range of 200-400 miles.&nbsp; (But then again... they didn't have someone named <b>Tony Stark</b> aboard, <i>heh</i>!)\n\n[HANG0] * The Thing/Hulk battle claimed at least one building, a bus, a Jeep, someone's speed boat and a good bit of one NYC's suspension bridges (the \"Washington bridge\" we find out later), not to mention part of one abandoned building and another abandoned warehouse (lucky all these \"abandoned\" structures seem to litter the streets of NYC!)."
  ],
  "letters_page": "[DEVNOTE: shellhead]\n\nMarc Bailey refers to Iron Man (while discussing <i>Tales of Suspense</i>) as \"Shell Head\" -- in quotes.&nbsp; The response from Marvel asks for \"more respect for our heroes\" and that they don't call him \"shell-head.\"&nbsp; But remember that the Hulk already called Iron Man by that name in [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08], so it's unlikely that Marc Bailey actually coined the name.\n\nRichard O'Baid's letter cracked me up when he pointed out that his doctor, making a house call, was distracted by a copy of [LINK:1963:XMEN_2|XMEN #2|2027-01-17] lying nearby while taking his temperature and indicated \"I'm going to tell my son to start buying it so I can read it too.\"&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; fonttype=italic; text=Amazing!]",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}